for sure. here’s a thread-ready version that keeps the point but doesn’t sound like it escaped a media studies class:

“the weirdest thing alien: earth is doing with synths is making them feel like foster kids and weapons at the same time. everybody wants to ‘raise’ them, manage them, use them, or claim them, but nobody can agree if they’re people or tools. that’s what makes it hit different.

it reminds me of david in prometheus, where the ‘son’ is really just property with daddy issues and a god complex. then you’ve got ripley and newt in aliens, where family isn’t blood or programming, it’s who actually protects you when everything goes bad.

so the synth stuff feels like it’s sitting between those two ideas. are they gonna become david because nobody loved them right, or can they become something else because somebody treats them like more than hardware? that foster-kid/weapon tension is the whole creepy little engine for me.”

if you want it sharper, you could cut it down to:

“alien: earth making synths feel like foster kids and weapons at the same time is nasty work. david shows what happens when a created ‘child’ gets treated like property. ripley/newt shows family as protection, not ownership. the show feels stuck right between those two versions of family.”
